{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2564 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b98aa8b914532f2d5e6f24cf7972310dc6f9c2bbce5bc8c58fd6577e84f82821" :edb/db-before-t 2563 :edb/db-after-t 2564 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535876 50 "2026-10-15T03:25:35.132Z" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186058674 1078 17592186058679 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186059476 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c039d0cdc7d0f127cdca06652328e84a458faaeedd4d6e610f452b0e93ba5c21.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186059476 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex is at the origin.\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c039d0cdc7d0f127cdca06652328e84a458faaeedd4d6e610f452b0e93ba5c21.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186059477 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees.\n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{2\\pi} 3 \\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = -120^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $-120^\\circ$.\n\nThen, recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and its vertex is at the origin.\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/f9/f920f36cc5dcbbfaa94f901a21bf5c19f4dc6846542d3d3c00ccbd5631a6362f.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186059477 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{2\\pi} 3 \\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \n\t-120^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $-\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ is equivalent to $-120^\\circ.$\n\nThen, recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/f9/f920f36cc5dcbbfaa94f901a21bf5c19f4dc6846542d3d3c00ccbd5631a6362f.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186059478 1074 "First, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex lies at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/33/3304f581480780d7139bac50ded646f78a8c8c344c0184ad81955bcc83acbe72.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186059478 1074 "First, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex lies at the origin.\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/33/3304f581480780d7139bac50ded646f78a8c8c344c0184ad81955bcc83acbe72.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186059648 1038 17592186535922 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186059648 1038 17592186535923 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186059649 1038 17592186535990 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186059650 1038 17592186535956 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186059650 1038 17592186535961 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186059864 1038 17592186535945 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186059864 1038 17592186535966 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186059865 1038 17592186535979 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186059865 1038 17592186535994 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186059895 1050 420.0 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186060342 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186060342 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows the unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the standard position?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186060342 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows the unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $-\\frac{π}{4}$ in the standard position?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186060342 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is equivalent to $-{45}^{∘}.$\n\nThen, recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/a3/a3106bc6788215147adfed66ac038523a73191a9b0182f35089bd1d7b2f87787.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186060878 1064 "Recall that, in the coordinate plane, an angle $\\theta$ in the standard position has its vertex at the origin, its initial side lies along the positive $x$-axis, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nSince a positive angle is always measured counter-clockwise, this means that a negative angle is measured *clockwise*.\n\nFor example, we can represent the angles\n\n$$\n\\theta = -45^\\circ, -135^\\circ, -225^\\circ\n$$\n\nand $-315^\\circ$ as central angles of the unit circle (in the standard way) as follows:\n\n![](images/0b/0b33ba078eecacd9f8d40622fb21be5ec710a22b6e69458006c5d9422441285a.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186060878 1064 "Recall that, in the coordinate plane, an angle $\\theta$ in the standard position has its vertex at the origin, its initial side lies along the positive $x$-axis, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nSince a positive angle is always measured counter-clockwise, this means that a negative angle is measured *clockwise*.\n\nFor example, we can represent the angles $\\theta = -45^\\circ, -135^\\circ, -225^\\circ,$ and $-315^\\circ$ as central angles of the unit circle (in the standard way) as follows:\n\n![](images/0b/0b33ba078eecacd9f8d40622fb21be5ec710a22b6e69458006c5d9422441285a.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186060879 1064 "Sometimes, we wish to represent negative angles in the coordinate plane without reference to the unit circle.\n\nAs with positive angles, the convention for representing negative angles is the same as without the unit circle.\n\nFor example, we can represent the angles\n\n$$\n\\theta = -45^\\circ, -135^\\circ, -225^\\circ\n$$\n\nand $-315^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane in the standard way as follows:\n\n![](images/01/019d5fc186f3c540702951b08d8dfcaf67cb82333a6c18a466dc21794e6dc94c.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186060879 1064 "Sometimes, we wish to represent negative angles in the coordinate plane without reference to the unit circle.\n\nAs with positive angles, the convention for representing negative angles is the same as without the unit circle.\n\nFor example, we can represent the angles $\\theta = -45^\\circ, -135^\\circ, -225^\\circ,$ and $-315^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane in the standard way as follows:\n\n![](images/01/019d5fc186f3c540702951b08d8dfcaf67cb82333a6c18a466dc21794e6dc94c.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186073746 1038 17592186535924 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186073746 1038 17592186535932 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186073747 1038 17592186535973 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078367 1071 "Find the image of the point $(-5,2)$ under the action of a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $-3$?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186078367 1071 "Find the image of the point $(-5,2)$ under the action of a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $-3?$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078367 1074 "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $-3$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (-3x,-3y)\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(-5,2)$, we have\n\n$$\n(-5,2) \\mapsto \\big((-3) \\cdot (-5), \\: (-3) \\cdot 2 \\big) = (15,-6)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(15,-6)$." 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186078367 1074 "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $-3$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (-3x,-3y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(-5,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n(-5,2) \\mapsto \\big((-3) \\cdot (-5), \\: (-3) \\cdot 2 \\big) = (15,-6).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(15,-6).$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078368 1071 "![](images/83/83e7c4533710e42e16cbe883188f619321914815d905344547bd6c81d253a3ac.png)\n\nWhat is the image of the polygon shown above under a dilation that is centered at the origin and has a scale factor of $-2$?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186078368 1071 "![](images/83/83e7c4533710e42e16cbe883188f619321914815d905344547bd6c81d253a3ac.png)\n\nWhat is the image of the polygon shown above under a dilation that is centered at the origin and has a scale factor of $-2?$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078368 1074 "The dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $-2$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(-2x,-2y\\right)\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the vertices $(2,1)$, $(3,1)$, $(3,3)$, $(1,3)$, $(1,2)$, and $(2,2)$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(2, 1) & ↦ (-2 \\cdot 2,-2 \\cdot 1) = (-4,-2) \\\\ (3, 1) & ↦ (-2 \\cdot 3,-2 \\cdot 1) = (-6,-2) \\\\ (3, 3) & ↦ (-2 \\cdot 3,-2 \\cdot 3) = (-6,-6) \\\\ (1, 3) & ↦ (-2 \\cdot 1,-2 \\cdot 3) = (-2,-6) \\\\ (1, 2) & ↦ (-2 \\cdot 1,-2 \\cdot 2) = (-2,-4) \\\\ (2, 2) & ↦ (-2 \\cdot 2,-2 \\cdot 2) = (-4,-4)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following polygon.\n\n![](images/a3/a3ce2ccd16db3e9013947b475bbca5fec1d34ff5e10414eff426e8389271c219.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186078368 1074 "The dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $-2$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(-2x,-2y\\right).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the vertices $(2,1),$ $(3,1),$ $(3,3),$  $(1,3),$ $(1,2),$  and $(2,2),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2,1) & ↦(-2\\cdot 2,\\,-2\\cdot 1)=(-4,-2) \\\\ (3,1) & ↦(-2\\cdot 3,\\,-2\\cdot 1)=(-6,-2) \\\\ (3,3) & ↦(-2\\cdot 3,\\,-2\\cdot 3)=(-6,-6) \\\\ (1,3) & ↦(-2\\cdot 1,\\,-2\\cdot 3)=(-2,-6) \\\\ (1,2) & ↦(-2\\cdot 1,\\,-2\\cdot 2)=(-2,-4) \\\\ (2,2) & ↦(-2\\cdot 2,\\,-2\\cdot 2)=(-4,-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following polygon.\n\n![](images/a3/a3ce2ccd16db3e9013947b475bbca5fec1d34ff5e10414eff426e8389271c219.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078369 1071 "![](images/cd/cd07f68993f7e151e2b24fe9305b726ce40b9bc5f56399d6616e46c83f90cc87.png)\n\n \n\nThe point $P'(-2,-4)$ is the image of the point $P(1,2)$ under a dilation with the center at the origin, as shown above. What is the scale factor of the transformation?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078369 1071 "![](images/cd/cd07f68993f7e151e2b24fe9305b726ce40b9bc5f56399d6616e46c83f90cc87.png)\n\nThe point $P'(-2,-4)$ is the image of the point $P(1,2)$ under a dilation with the center at the origin, as shown above. What is the scale factor of the transformation?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186078369 1074 "A dilation with center at the origin and a factor of $k$ can be represented by the function\n$(x,y) \\mapsto (kx,ky)\\,$.\n\nApplying this function to the point $P$ and equating the result to $P'$, we get\n\n$$\n(k \\cdot 1, k \\cdot 2) = (-2,-4)⟹{k =-2; 2k =-4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac{(-4)}{2}=-2\n$$\n\n![](images/21/219980e6dee08094db21c153f37b107d6fd85709aa50cf8c546a5ba050aac567.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186078369 1074 "A dilation with center at the origin and a factor of $k$ can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (kx,ky)\\,.\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $P$ and equating the result to $P',$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k\\cdot 1,k\\cdot 2)=(-2,-4)\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}k=-2 \\\\ 2k=-4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\dfrac{(-4)}{2}=-2.$\n\n![](images/21/219980e6dee08094db21c153f37b107d6fd85709aa50cf8c546a5ba050aac567.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078373 1071 "![](images/94/94ff7096b1befc0ce2538efc345ff3f090c66ee28f98bf62b6162cfed9226137.png)\n\nA dilation with center $O$ is defined by the function $(x,y)  \\mapsto \\left(\\dfrac53x, \\dfrac53y\\right).$  What is the image of the line shown above under the action of this transformation?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078373 1071 "![](images/94/94ff7096b1befc0ce2538efc345ff3f090c66ee28f98bf62b6162cfed9226137.png)\n\nA dilation with center $O$ is defined by the function $(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(\\dfrac53x, \\dfrac53y\\right)$. What is the image of the line shown above under the action of this transformation?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186078373 1074 "A dilation maps a line $l$ not passing through the center of the dilation onto a parallel line $l'$.\n\nTo find the image of the line, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Take a point $B \\in l$.\n- Find the image $B'$ of $B$ under the dilation.\n- Find $l'$ as the line that passes through $B'$ and is parallel to $l$.\n\nNotice that $B(-3,0)$ lies on $l$, as shown below. The image of this point under the dilation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{5}{3} \\cdot (-3), \\frac{5}{3} \\cdot 0) &= (-5, 0)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following image of $l$:\n\n![](images/7e/7eca5ac26e32d153f69f67076100c7c1ebc26efe26d5b1603ed459a9101f93db.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186078373 1074 "A dilation maps a line $l$ not passing through the center of the dilation onto a parallel line $l'.$ \n\nTo find the image of the line, we proceed as follows: \n\n- Take a point $B \\in l.$\n\n- Find the image $B'$ of $B$ under the dilation.\n\n- Find $l'$ as the line that passes through $B'$ and is parallel to $l.$\n\nNotice that $B(-3,0)$ lies on $l,$ as shown below. The image of this point under the dilation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot (-3),\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 0) & =(-5,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following image of $l\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/7e/7eca5ac26e32d153f69f67076100c7c1ebc26efe26d5b1603ed459a9101f93db.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078464 1038 17592186535942 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078464 1038 17592186535978 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078465 1038 17592186535958 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078517 1038 17592186535953 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078517 1038 17592186535992 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078518 1038 17592186535980 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078518 1038 17592186535997 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078519 1038 17592186535927 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078519 1038 17592186535948 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078520 1038 17592186535925 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078520 1038 17592186535935 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186078625 1050 660.0 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186079678 1064 "In the coordinate plane, dilations are typically centered at the origin. When a dilation is centered at the origin, it can be represented by a function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (kx, ky)\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the scale factor of the dilation.\n\nFor example, let's compute the image of the point $(3,2)$ (shown below) under the action of a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $3$.\n\n![](images/2d/2da731925da5705e15cb58abd632e52c04139e8052772eeaa3ca2862fd6d3d02.png)\n\nA dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $3$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(3x,3y\\right)\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(3,2)$, we have\n\n$$\n(3,2) \\mapsto \\left(3\\cdot 3, \\:3 \\cdot 2 \\right) = (9,6)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(9,6)$.\n\n![](images/94/9410030b5b309d512c937741e9fdb85abad0aacdd712f81ee0b50897523952cc.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186079678 1064 "In the coordinate plane, dilations are typically centered at the origin. When a dilation is centered at the origin, it can be represented by a function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (kx, ky)\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the scale factor of the dilation.\n\nFor example, let's compute the image of the point $(3,2)$ (shown below) under the action of a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $3.$ \n\n![](images/2d/2da731925da5705e15cb58abd632e52c04139e8052772eeaa3ca2862fd6d3d02.png)\n\nA dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $3$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(3x,3y\\right).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(3,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n(3,2) \\mapsto \\left(3\\cdot 3, \\:3 \\cdot 2 \\right) = (9,6).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(9,6).$\n\n![](images/94/9410030b5b309d512c937741e9fdb85abad0aacdd712f81ee0b50897523952cc.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186079679 1064 "If $l$ is a line passing through the center of dilation, then the image $l'$ under the dilation is the same as the original line.\n\n![](images/eb/eb208ea1b492a1d471e0ffb6b3f23fa80a4747f3b5f6da1ad126c30716e52743.png)\n\nHowever, if $k$ is a line that does not pass through the center of dilation, then the image $k'$ is parallel to the original line.\n\n![](images/09/09a11211bde36f85dfe48e4a769314d084edc99c523b869ed3f9a518784fb9a6.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186079679 1064 "If $l$ is a line passing through the center of dilation, then the image $l'$ under the dilation is the same as the original line. \n\n![](images/eb/eb208ea1b492a1d471e0ffb6b3f23fa80a4747f3b5f6da1ad126c30716e52743.png)\n\nHowever, if $k$ is a line that does not pass through the center of dilation, then the image $k'$ is parallel to the original line.\n\n![](images/09/09a11211bde36f85dfe48e4a769314d084edc99c523b869ed3f9a518784fb9a6.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186079703 1064 "Let's discuss how to find a center of a dilation\n\nThe center of a dilation lies on any line that passes through corresponding points on a figure and its image. Therefore, we can find a center of dilation as the intersection of any two of these lines.\n\nLet's look at some examples:\n\n- Consider the rectangle $ABCD$ and its image $A'B'C'D'$ under the action of a dilation, shown below.![](images/91/9115c6a43d20f58e8c2e4b9a77337edd18688dd831168405062c6e0fbe207bb0.png)\nWe can find the center of dilation $O$ by connecting two corresponding points with a straight line and finding their intersection.\n\nFor example, if we draw the lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AA'}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BB'}$ and find their intersection, we get the center of dilation $O$ shown below.![](images/4a/4a3b1ac04e7d549aafdcf50ea55e172f385fbb7a71bd061c74581ca50f94535a.png)\n\n- Sometimes, a figure and its image lie on opposite sides of the center of dilation. For example, consider the rectangle $ABCD$ and its image $A'B'C'D'$ shown below.![](images/a8/a8918779d1b5152e7071bdb7ba7d349978b166ff613ec2c8b918f9c2ea5b0b92.png)\nIf we draw the lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AA'}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BB'}$, we find that the center of dilation $O$ lies between the two shapes.![](images/14/14c6bc0bd148d2ac61c42d4e1636c4301a7af77e773338358e51e4a297f40a48.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186079703 1064 "Let's discuss how to find a center of a dilation\n\nThe center of a dilation lies on any line that passes through corresponding points on a figure and its image. Therefore, we can find a center of dilation as the intersection of any two of these lines. \n\nLet's look at some examples:\n\n- Consider the rectangle $ABCD$ and its image $A'B'C'D'$ under the action of a dilation, shown below.\n\n![](images/91/9115c6a43d20f58e8c2e4b9a77337edd18688dd831168405062c6e0fbe207bb0.png)\n\nWe can find the center of dilation $O$ by connecting two corresponding points with a straight line and finding their intersection. \n\nFor example, if we draw the lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AA'}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BB'}$ and find their intersection, we get the center of dilation $O$ shown below.\n\n![](images/4a/4a3b1ac04e7d549aafdcf50ea55e172f385fbb7a71bd061c74581ca50f94535a.png)\n\n- Sometimes, a figure and its image lie on opposite sides of the center of dilation. \n\nFor example, consider the rectangle $ABCD$ and its image $A'B'C'D'$ shown below. \n\n![](images/a8/a8918779d1b5152e7071bdb7ba7d349978b166ff613ec2c8b918f9c2ea5b0b92.png)\n\nIf we draw the lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AA'}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BB'},$ we find that the center of dilation $O$ lies between the two shapes.\n\n![](images/14/14c6bc0bd148d2ac61c42d4e1636c4301a7af77e773338358e51e4a297f40a48.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186080981 1078 17592186080983 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186082295 1078 17592186082297 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186083166 1038 17592186536004 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186083167 1038 17592186535959 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186083185 1038 17592186535936 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186083675 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186083675 1071 "The table below defines the function $f(x)$. Given that $f(x)$ is an even function, find the value of $a + b$.\n\n| **$x$** | $-23$ | $-15$ | $-7$ | $7$ | $15$ | $23$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $a + 5$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $b + 1$ | $4$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $6$ |" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186083675 1071 "The table below defines the function $f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is an even function, find the value of $a+b.$\n\n| **$x$** | $-23$ | $-15$ | $-7$ | $7$ | $15$ | $23$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $a+5$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $b+1$ | $4$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $6$ |" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186083675 1074 "Since $f(x)$ is an even function, we must have that $f(-x)=f(x).$ \n\nUsing the fact that $f(-23)=f(23),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-23) & =f(23) \\\\ a+5 & =6 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $f(-7)=f(7),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-7) & =f(7) \\\\ b+1 & =4 \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=1+3=4.\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186083894 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186083894 1071 "Is the function $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{2}}$, for $x \\ne 0$, even, odd, or neither?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186083894 1071 "Is the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$, for $x\\ne 0$, even, odd, or neither?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186083894 1074 "Remember that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x,$ while\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x.$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{1}{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=f(x),$ the function is even.\n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne -f(x),$ the function is not odd.\n\nTherefore, the function is only even." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186087800 1038 17592186535946 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186087800 1038 17592186535952 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186087801 1038 17592186535964 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186087801 1038 17592186535987 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186087802 1038 17592186535931 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186087802 1038 17592186535947 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186087802 1038 17592186535971 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186087940 1050 840.0 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186094867 1075 17592186094868 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [175