ted $2\\pi$ radians about the line $y=1$. Find the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n![](images/36/362a92640ed68dfd5fa85f0338d95233003e9f8a1b059a5dffa2cda243a1411b.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186096531 1071 "The region below enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt x$ and $y=x$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $y=1.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid. \n\n![](images/36/362a92640ed68dfd5fa85f0338d95233003e9f8a1b059a5dffa2cda243a1411b.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186096531 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x & =\\sqrt x \\\\[3pt] x-\\sqrt x & = 0 \\\\[3pt] \\sqrt x \\left(\\sqrt x - 1\\right) &= 0 \\\\[3pt] x& = 0, 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$.\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/54/5456bed18c03e823c8e9388571b5671f5f476f5caa1085793314b57080b2b07c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$, and\n- *leaves* though the outer curver $y=x$.\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=x$.![](images/51/5136903876ed5ea811ebf28e0da905b5029b2c9311527fce9d0fed8e7bd1dd46.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{outer} &= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(upper) - (lower)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(1) - (x)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[1 - x]^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$.![](images/c8/c850643bb1c315d0516eff55d6270078504c1c98687cfac40e0c9b4b2c771b7c.png)\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n$V_{inner}|= π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[(upper) - (lower)]^{2}dx; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[(1) - (\\sqrt{x})]^{2}dx; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[1 - \\sqrt{x}]^{2}dx$.\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.Finally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n$\\begin{aligned} V &= {\\color{blue}V_{\\text{outer}}} - {\\color{red}V_{\\text{inner}}} \\\\[5pt] & = \\pi \\displaystyle \\int_0^1 [1-x]^2 - \\pi \\displaystyle \\int_0^1 [1-\\sqrt x]^2\\,\\textrm d x \\\\[5pt] & = \\pi \\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\left((1-x)^2 - (1-\\sqrt x)^2\\right) \\,\\textrm d x \\\\[5pt] & = \\pi \\displaystyle \\int_0^1 (x^2-3x+2\\sqrt x) \\,\\,\\textrm d x \\\\[5pt] & = \\pi\\left. \\left(\\dfrac {1} 3 x^3 - \\dfrac{3}{2} x^2 + \\dfrac{4}{3} x^{3/2} \\ \\right)\\right|_0^1 \\\\[5pt] & = \\pi \\left[\\left(\\dfrac {1}3 (1)^3 - \\dfrac{3}{2}(1)^2 + \\dfrac{4}{3}(1)^{3/2} \\right) - (0) \\right] \\\\[5pt] & = \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{1}3 - \\dfrac{3}{2} +\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac {\\pi}{6}. \\end{aligned}$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186096531 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n \t\tx & =\\sqrt x  \\[3pt]\n \t\tx-\\sqrt x & = 0 \\[3pt]\n \t\t\\sqrt x \\left(\\sqrt x - 1\\right) &= 0 \\[3pt]\n \t\t\tx& = 0, 1\n\t}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/54/5456bed18c03e823c8e9388571b5671f5f476f5caa1085793314b57080b2b07c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ and\n\n- *leaves* though the outer curver $y=x.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=x.$\n\n![](images/51/5136903876ed5ea811ebf28e0da905b5029b2c9311527fce9d0fed8e7bd1dd46.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(1)-(x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n![](images/c8/c850643bb1c315d0516eff55d6270078504c1c98687cfac40e0c9b4b2c771b7c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(1)-(\\sqrt{x})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1-\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nV &= {\\color{blue}V_{\\text{outer}}} - {\\color{brown}V_{\\text{inner}}} \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi \\int_0^1 [1-x]^2 - \\pi \\int_0^1 [1-\\sqrt x]^2\\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi \\int_0^1 \\left((1-x)^2 - (1-\\sqrt x)^2\\right) \\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi \\int_0^1 (x^2-3x+2\\sqrt x) \\,\\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\pi\\left. \\left( \\dfrac {1} 3 x^3 - \\dfrac{3}{2} x^2 + \\dfrac{4}{3} x^{3/2 } \\ \\right)\\right|_0^1\\[5pt] \n \t\t& =  \\pi \\left[\\left( \\dfrac {1}3 (1)^3 - \\dfrac{3}{2}(1)^2  + \\dfrac{4 }{3}(1)^{3/2 } \\right) - (0) \\right]\\[5pt]\n \t\t& =  \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{1}3 - \\dfrac{3}{2} +\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\\[5pt]\n \t\t& = \\dfrac {\\pi}{6} .\n \t\t}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186096532 1071 "The region enclosed by the curves $x=y^2$ and $x=2-y^2$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=-1$. Find the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n![](images/e8/e8f0f999521058f3213e11bd3df67a95a6ea8604e6853d06923ffbba6cf9d17c.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186096532 1071 "The region enclosed by the curves $x=y^2$ and $x=2-y^2$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=-1.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n![](images/e8/e8f0f999521058f3213e11bd3df67a95a6ea8604e6853d06923ffbba6cf9d17c.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186096532 1074 "First, to determine the limits of integration, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 2 - y^{2} \\\\\n2y^{2} &= 2 \\\\\ny^{2} &= 1 \\\\\ny &= ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=1$.\n\nNext, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/c3/c3c9217600a26e11614752b2ca2ac8b3aa6c12b0df9f3a8c13d2b32c5b338107.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=y^2$, and\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $x={2-y^2}$.\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of outer and inner solids.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={2-y^2}$.![](images/36/3696bac064f5157b5c4ab3aefe34128d2d126a0a6b586106eb1e439bc90e7df5.png)\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n$V_{outer}|= π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[(right) - (left)]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[(2 - y^{2}) - (-1)]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[3 - y^{2}]^{2}dy$.\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y^2$.![](images/f5/f59ee5966f0cb9572d00e7f7278a7462841aef6b18f4865924303ad65b50738c.png)\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n$V_{inner}|= π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[(right) - (left)]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[(y^{2}) - (-1)]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[y^{2} + 1]^{2}dy$.\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.Finally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n$V|= V_{outer} - V_{inner}; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[3 - y^{2}]^{2}dx - π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[y^{2} + 1]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}([3 - y^{2}]^{2} - [y^{2} + 1]^{2})dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}(8 - 8y^{2})dy; = \\left.π(8y - \\frac{8y^{3}}{3})\\right|_{-1}^{1}; = π[(8 - \\frac{8}{3}) - (-8 + \\frac{8}{3})]; = π(\\frac{32}{3}); = \\frac{32π}{3}$." 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186096532 1074 "First, to determine the limits of integration, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=1.$\n\nNext, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/c3/c3c9217600a26e11614752b2ca2ac8b3aa6c12b0df9f3a8c13d2b32c5b338107.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=y^2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $x={2-y^2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of outer and inner solids.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={2-y^2}.$\n\n![](images/36/3696bac064f5157b5c4ab3aefe34128d2d126a0a6b586106eb1e439bc90e7df5.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\,(2-{y}^{2})-(-1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[3-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y^2.$\n\n![](images/f5/f59ee5966f0cb9572d00e7f7278a7462841aef6b18f4865924303ad65b50738c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,({y}^{2})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{y}^{2}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[3-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{y}^{2}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[3-{y}^{2}]}^{2}-{[{y}^{2}+1]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(8-8{y}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(8y-\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3})|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(8-\\frac{8}{3})-(-8+\\frac{8}{3})] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{32}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186096569 1074 "First, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/59/59041e6817cbf26f9de00de0ec864cea57f1a419877fe5c3203857c5488c7324.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=-2x$, and\n- *leaves* though the outer curver $y=2x^2$.\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=2x^2$.![](images/07/0761073a5f7f417a07f35cb0b7c08dd0b4ea6bd84da71f3e474911ee036c546b.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{outer} &= π\\int_{-1}^{0}[(upper) - (lower)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{0}[(3) - (2x^{2})]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{0}[3 - 2x^{2}]^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=-2x$.\n\n![](images/9e/9ea5e5dd50dcd623532557e5365118ae5c9b950be059ba8c0069c7b36071f2c3.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{inner} &= π\\int_{-1}^{0}[(upper) - (lower)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{0}[(3) - (-2x)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{0}[3 + 2x]^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- *