he point $A$. To do this, we need to plot a new point $A'$ on the opposite side of $l$ at an equal distance away from $l$ as the original point $A$.\n\nThe original point $A$ is $4$ units to the left of $l$, so we need to plot the new point $A'$ at a distance of $4$ units to the right of $l$.\n\n![](images/33/33bb82c9d0f20df13f46060b9aec21e8c4d1231766926c47b7718bf58e1c5e32.png)\n\nNow let's reflect other vertices of the quadrilateral in the same way:\n\n![](images/00/00ec763ccce462effa024486a6ff09a98b549c61179de18bb850470e835a7627.png)\n\nFinally, we draw segments between the reflected vertices.\n\n![](images/7f/7ffd0cfa45051d8376c46528370b34222a1ebb226987e7530d5081d69942bbde.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186173142 1064 "We can reflect an object in any line. Consider the diagram below. The two polygons are the mirror images of each other in the line $l.$\n\n![](images/1e/1e4dfb2939916df5d5018320819f14fcec261a1e77c171b551e9868a8c3bebc8.png)\n\nIf we want to reflect the quadrilateral $ABCD$ below in the line $l,$ we can reflect each vertex separately and then draw the segments between the resulting corresponding vertices.\n\n![](images/a4/a47c41b41ecae2bc4c281e1b90c843ae68fa907a0d2ff49c48454fd80e2977c7.png)\n\nLet's start by reflecting the point $A.$ To do this, we need to plot a new point $A'$ on the opposite side of $l$ at an equal distance away from $l$ as the original point $A.$\n\nThe original point $A$ is $4$ units to the left of $l,$ so we need to plot the new point $A'$ at a distance of $4$ units to the right of $l.$\n\n![](images/33/33bb82c9d0f20df13f46060b9aec21e8c4d1231766926c47b7718bf58e1c5e32.png)\n\nNow let's reflect other vertices of the quadrilateral in the same way:\n\n![](images/00/00ec763ccce462effa024486a6ff09a98b549c61179de18bb850470e835a7627.png)\n\nFinally, we draw segments between the reflected vertices.\n\n![](images/7f/7ffd0cfa45051d8376c46528370b34222a1ebb226987e7530d5081d69942bbde.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186173143 1064 "If we're given two points, we can find the line of reflection that maps one point onto another by finding the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining the two points.\n\nFor example, suppose that the line $l$ reflects the point $A$ onto $A'$ below. How can we find the equation of $l$?\n\n![](images/79/79ddc15badc704a9c9860207a5156c3a0505ab626572581bcf3146976338a015.png)\n\nThe line $l$ is the perpendicular bisector of the line segment $\\overline{AA'}$. So, the line of reflection is $y=-1$.\n\n![](images/aa/aa240e562acf388bdf05751e680e917bc8465bad34300334f6b423df46a7d1f3.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186173143 1064 "If we're given two points, we can find the line of reflection that maps one point onto another by finding the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining the two points.\n\nFor example, suppose that the line $l$ reflects the point $A$ onto $A'$ below. How can we find the equation of $l?$\n\n![](images/79/79ddc15badc704a9c9860207a5156c3a0505ab626572581bcf3146976338a015.png)\n\nThe line $l$ is the perpendicular bisector of the line segment $\\overline{AA'}.$ So, the line of reflection is $y=-1.$\n\n![](images/aa/aa240e562acf388bdf05751e680e917bc8465bad34300334f6b423df46a7d1f3.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186175936 1038 17592186535999 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186175937 1038 17592186535993 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186179728 1038 17592186535937 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186179728 1038 17592186536002 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186180010 1068 17592186177729 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186187566 1075 17592186187567 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186187566 1075 17592186187568 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186187566 1075 17592186187569 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186187566 1075 17592186187570 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186187566 1075 17592186187571 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186187566 1075 17592186535436 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186187566 1075 17592186535527 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186187566 1075 17592186535635 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186187566 1075 17592186535800 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186187566 1075 17592186535813 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186187566 1078 17592186535635 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186188257 1038 17592186535974 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186188291 1038 17592186535968 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186188915 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186188915 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative $y^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the distance from $P$ to the origin. What differential equation describes this curve?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186188915 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the distance from $P$ to the origin. What differential equation describes this curve?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186188915 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **distance from $P$ to the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *distance from $P(x,y)$ to the origin* is given by $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the distance from $P$ to the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186199484 1064 "Suppose we want to find the minimum and maximum values of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to constraint $g(x,y) = x^2+y^2 \\leq 1.$ In other words, we wish to find the global extrema of $f(x,y)$ defined on the disc of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5ac34145b3911b19eb3775a915e8f0462c78502be6d6292e7682265c86eafce7.png)\n\n  \n\nTo find the extrema, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the critical points of $f$ inside the domain $x^2+y^2 < 1.$\n\n- Then, we find the extreme values of $f$ subject to the constraint $x^2+y^2=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers.\n\n- Finally, we determine the maximum and minimum values by evaluating $f$ at the candidate points.\n\nRecall that the critical points of a function $f(x,y)$ are the solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ Now, since the derivative of $f$ is the transpose of its gradient $\\nabla f,$ the critical points are also given by the solutions to $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0.$\n\nSo, we can find the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ Note that this point belongs to our domain.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + y^2  = 1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1), \\qquad (0,0).\n$$\n\nNote that we add the critical point $(0,0)$ to the list of the candidates above.\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0, \\pm 1)$ | $(0, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $0$ at the point $(0, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at the points $(0, \\pm 1).$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 false] [17592186199484 1064 "Suppose we want to find the minimum and maximum values of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to constraint $g(x,y) = x^2+y^2 \\leq 1.$ In other words, we wish to find the global extrema of $f(x,y)$ defined on the disc of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5ac34145b3911b19eb3775a915e8f0462c78502be6d6292e7682265c86eafce7.png)\n\n  \n\nTo find the extrema, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the critical points of $f$ inside the domain $x^2+y^2 < 1.$\n\n- Then, we find the extreme values of $f$ subject to the constraint $x^2+y^2=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers.\n\n- Finally, we determine the maximum and minimum values by evaluating $f$ at the candidate points.\n\nRecall that the critical points of a function $f(x,y)$ are the solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ Now, since the derivative of $f$ is the transpose of its gradient $\\nabla f,$ the critical points are also given by the solutions to $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0.$\n\nSo, we can find the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ Note that this point belongs to our domain.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + y^2  = 1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1), \\qquad (0,0).\n$$\n\nNote that we add the critical point $(0,0)$ to the list of the candidates above.\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0, \\pm 1)$ | $(0, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $0$ at the point $(0, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at the points $(0, \\pm 1).$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [175