7 true] [17592186535921 1075 17592186535812 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535921 1076 17592186045493 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535921 1077 "selection" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535921 1078 17592186535579 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535921 1079 #uuid "f7f1648e-bfb1-591c-80ea-4405790435c4" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535922 1068 17592186535884 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535922 1069 "q-124381" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535922 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535922 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $-{290}^{∘}$ in the standard position?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535922 1072 #uuid "04667e51-0e45-5016-8605-f4f7cb07f685" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535922 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, a vertex that lies at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/11/11463517601b3a41a93fb396861b5a302c1ffdf3d32310d50460eaa358d16177.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535923 1068 17592186535862 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535923 1069 "q-124378" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535923 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535923 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $-{310}^{∘}$ in the standard position?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535923 1072 #uuid "0507850a-add7-5189-ad79-a9f8fe7e7fce" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535923 1074 "Recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, a vertex that lies at the origin. It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/c2/c2aa0f0b3bd88556e29ae859a9feae21332b58bb06dbe9e27ca7c1d639feb9e0.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535924 1068 17592186535921 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535924 1069 "q-14005" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535924 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535924 1071 "Determine the real roots of the cubic equation $10{y}^{3}-y=2{y}^{3}+y.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535924 1072 #uuid "05ed837d-3821-5be3-89df-8ca589e409e2" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535924 1074 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(y)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{y}^{3}-y & =2{y}^{3}+y \\\\ 8{y}^{3}-y & =y \\\\ 8{y}^{3}-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $2y.$ We can factor out the $2y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{y}^{3}-2y & =0 \\\\ 2y(4{y}^{2}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $4{y}^{2}-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y(4{y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 2y(2y+1)(2y-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $y=0,$ $z=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535925 1068 17592186535868 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535925 1069 "q-125817" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535925 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535925 1071 "![](images/3c/3c90c2114f36f77c9bb06c47398f4f5a4ce3b8fc6aadf2a77d28c447a8e424c7.png)\n\nA dilation with center $O$ is defined by the function $(x,y)↦(1.5x,1.5y).$  What is the image of the line shown above under the action of this transformation?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535925 1072 #uuid "072c2798-6d16-5d14-9659-f337b6ce7515" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535925 1074 "A dilation leaves a line passing through the center of the dilation unchanged.\n\nTherefore, the image ${l}^{′}$ of the line $l$ under the dilation  $(x,y)↦(1.5x,1.5y)$ is the line $l$ itself, as shown below. \n\n![](images/b4/b48fecb9d9286ac4df6a9bfbf151fd78a98d223f7375e5251cda1119f8f66611.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535926 1068 17592186535873 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535926 1069 "q-50324" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535926 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535926 1071 "![](images/b0/b0e8cbe1a26b5a012f6098b67320070e882fa38783d8eda07dd0b76011a47b21.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{1}{y}$ and $x=\\frac{5}{2}-y$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535926 1072 #uuid "0ad0e9c9-a63e-55c4-a91b-b9b32de9ed5c" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535926 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y} & =\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{5}{2}y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-\\frac{5}{2}y+1 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-5y+2 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)(2y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2},2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ to $y=2.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/d5/d5067706f9ff1abbdfd9dfce94711cb854f79c1156ddc0d81c1c35a5ed555f7a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{1}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{2}-y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{2}-y.$\n\n![](images/a9/a944979bc352b0e40afea73836b22c1fb1513fabbc727fb60d4c96793ee6fbb5.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{5}{2}-y)-(-1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\frac{1}{y}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1eb04cf2f3ae0669dd82106b064dd822da618e9ca31bb3b96e4e30c71b7c3193.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{1}{y})-(-1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}({[\\frac{7}{2}-y]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{y}+1]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535927 1068 17592186535912 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535927 1069 "q-125779" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535927 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535927 1071 "![](images/14/14ca2d4167ff6950d5a9d7bb41cc856ecbc211a033518b8b366ac977953700b1.png)\n\nThe point ${P}^{′}(3,1.5)$ is the image of the point $P(-4,-2)$ under a dilation with the center at the origin, as shown above. What is the scale factor of the transformation?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535927 1072 #uuid "0b671036-c3a4-5237-8125-3af3f125754a" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535927 1074 "A dilation with center at the origin and a factor of $k$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(kx,ky)\\,.\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $P$ and equating the result to ${P}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k\\cdot (-4),k\\cdot (-2)=(3,1.5)\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-4k=3 \\\\ -2k=1.5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{3}{-4}=\\frac{1.5}{-2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$   \n\n![](images/d3/d3b818151bcfddd3fd4f642463ba4e06ba1844d4b772652dadde0d3049e441ee.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535928 1068 17592186535836 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535928 1069 "q-351346" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535928 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535928 1071 "![](images/64/64b0f84b62adc0db3385a75d8fdc6e6707eb6778084b9e21de863ca6954c4c1d.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+{y}^{2}=25$ and $x+5y=25$ for $y\\in [0,5]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=25.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535928 1072 #uuid "0c99954c-8e68-5e26-befe-7d930f5b079f" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535928 1074 "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2} & =25\\,⟹\\,x=25-{y}^{2} \\\\ x+5y & =25\\,⟹\\,x=25-5y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f5/f5b7cafc0ad6ab7624c1274451f16ba2c3c9a111ac9dad3abd90f082cc42d9b9.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=25-{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=25-5y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=25-5y.$\n\n![](images/16/166dbb3f75000dfc155abfc3452ff6874dab1542409edc7e4867c5a5ee73c0b0.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}{[\\,(25)-(25-5y)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}{[5y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=25-{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/93/9322b6f4bc9a0e913670f8dcbc9657206c3ae6a30de2f26308054d75b67d0861.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[\\,(25)-(25-{y}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}[{y}^{2}]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}({(5y)}^{2}-{({y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5}(25{y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{25{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{25(5)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(5)^{5}}{5})-0] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{3125}{3}-625) \\\\  & =\\frac{1250\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535929 1068 17592186535898 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535929 1069 "q-115697" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535929 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535929 1071 "Estimate the area under the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+2x$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535929 1072 #uuid "0fbb26d4-3faf-5389-98e4-54735a0cfc0b" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535929 1074 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/0b/0b9dd2388a7b60866eb15d336d51d181853fe5ac0e52dcd3e02f53fdd4848532.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.75$ | $1$ | $0.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(0.75+1+0.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535930 1068 17592186535918 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535930 1069 "q-20794" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535930 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535930 1071 "Estimate the area under the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535930 1072 #uuid "10bc8e6f-4c2c-5a63-aa64-53c9dbd12a9a" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535930 1074 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/5c/5c52bbe248df0e5a595298d978276023bcb681daa904f37e2818013687aab52e.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.75$ | $3$ | $1.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.75+3+1.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =8.50\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[0,2],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535931 1068 17592186535843 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535931 1069 "q-280382" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535931 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535931 1071 "Consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\nx=15{t}^{2},\\,y=3t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the parabola is\n\n${y}^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535931 1072 #uuid "117d2bbe-700d-5500-84d8-cda2c172b75c" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535931 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{15},\\,t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x}{15}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{x}{15} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{5}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{5}x.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535932 1068 17592186535839 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535932 1069 "q-38109" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535932 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535932 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $4{x}^{3}+3x=3{x}^{3}-4x.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535932 1072 #uuid "1444bcbd-c7af-503c-a5fd-f72a90d6d77a" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535932 1074 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(x)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}+3x & =3{x}^{3}-4x \\\\ {x}^{3}+3x & =-4x \\\\ {x}^{3}+7x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $x.$ We can factor out the $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+7x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}+7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{2}+7=0$ has no real solutions, we cannot factor the expression ${x}^{2}+7$  further.\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the only solution is $x=0.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535933 1068 17592186535882 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535933 1069 "q-74351" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535933 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535933 1071 "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[1,7].$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2.5$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $5$ | $8.5$ | $9$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[1,7].$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535933 1072 #uuid "16561015-1e48-5ef8-9194-b46c8f058fda" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535933 1074 "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈f(2.5)Δ{x}_{1}+f(5)Δ{x}_{2}+f(7)Δ{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2.5$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $-$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $5$ | $8.5$ | $9$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈5\\cdot 1.5+8.5\\cdot 2.5+9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7.5+21.25+18 \\\\  & =46.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535934 1068 17592186535838 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535934 1069 "q-316517" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535934 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535934 1071 "The following table shows the water flow rate, $R(t),$ through a pipe (in liters per minute) over a period of $40$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R\\,\\text{(liters/minute)}$ | $12$ | $15$ | $17$ | $16$ | $14$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum for $R(t)$ over the interval $[0,40]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total volume of water pumped.\n\nThe total volume is \n{{field-1}} liters." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535934 1072 #uuid "18df9f2e-656d-5486-9ae1-27119d2f448d" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535934 1074 "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=40,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{40-0}{4}=10.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{R(10)+R(20)+R(30)+R(40)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $R(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $15$ | $17$ | $16$ | $14$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(15+17+16+14)\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =62\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =620\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total volume of water pumped over a period of $40$ minutes. So, the total volume is $620$ liters." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535935 1068 17592186535867 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535935 1069 "q-89713" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535935 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535935 1071 "![](images/4c/4c3e53db0b578b306a12711c4e69a0d75b850cb1eecc8d5b0e6df79acc494d7a.png)\n\nA dilation with center $O$ is defined by the function $(x,y)↦(0.2x,0.2y).$  What is the image of the line shown above under the action of this transformation?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535935 1072 #uuid "19060a1c-49c3-55db-84f0-fb34722058a7" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535935 1074 "A dilation leaves a line passing through the center of the dilation unchanged.\n\nTherefore, the image ${l}^{′}$ of the line $l$ under the dilation  $(x,y)↦(0.2x,0.2y)$ is the line $l$ itself, as shown below. \n\n![](images/1a/1ab7188d164a491720d4d9d361abd4c2c76ea2cb09f6edbea3b0790a6281a785.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535936 1068 17592186535872 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535936 1069 "q-72131" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535936 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535936 1071 "Is the function $f(x)=6x$ even, odd, or neither?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535936 1072 #uuid "1b73902c-ad8b-52f6-bdea-d56e526c20d4" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535936 1074 "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =6(-x) \\\\  & =-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne f(x),$ the function is not even. \n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-(6x) \\\\  & =-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ the function is odd." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535937 1068 17592186535858 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535937 1069 "q-124659" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535937 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535937 1071 "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(1,3)$ to the point $N(3,-1),$ then ${\\int }_{C}{e}^{x-y}\\,dx+{e}^{x-y}\\,dy=$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535937 1072 #uuid "1c089741-5544-5275-8ceb-bbd95cf5e4fe" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535937 1074 "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=3\\,i-j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+t(3\\,i-j-(i+3\\,j)) \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+t(2\\,i-4\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(3-4t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t+1$ and $y(t)=3-4t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i-4\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x-y}\\,dx+{e}^{x-y}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+{e}^{x-y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x-y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+1-(3-4t)}(2+(-4))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{6t-2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{6t-2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[{e}^{6(1)-2}-{e}^{6(0)-2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[{e}^{4}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{6}}{3{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535938 1068 17592186535913 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535938 1069 "q-86453" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535938 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535938 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535938 1072 #uuid "21f7ba2a-9a0f-57d4-9aff-030342b38301" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535938 1074 "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the second of these integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u=1+{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(1+0)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535939 1068 17592186535897 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535939 1069 "q-102579" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535939 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535939 1071 "![](images/f6/f6ab47a6cf805bbae1e2b079239d716f0ffa94507df6c8c057547bc9b3848916.png)\n\nThe polygon $P$ is mapped to the polygon ${P}^{′}$ under the action of a reflection. What is the equation of the line of reflection?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535939 1072 #uuid "2390f18e-0af9-5fcf-b59a-c441026530cc" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535939 1074 "The line of reflection is the perpendicular bisector of the segments joining each point to its image. \n\nTherefore, the line of reflection is $y=1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f8abf0ecccfd5e1ead751263383f15d150e10d7ec4d972a88656512e0e09441f.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535940 1068 17592186535852 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535940 1069 "q-124728" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535940 1070 17592186045486 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535940 1071 "![](images/ef/efe8084c2e5b9d5eff4f75dffc9acce77aa66ab0dc62c699589573d96f884d49.png)\n\nThe region above enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-2$ and $y=-{x}^{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-2.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535940 1072 #uuid "25934858-d636-5a89-8459-35c28b65815f" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535940 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2 & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/45/4514019be21deb238c92f557cc041aec9b5e6629bc9d7dcac133a7aa2aed89c7.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y={x}^{2}-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=-{x}^{2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=-{x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/61/611d10f83c76467c69a1510809738611962dd5d1e1c22a10b0cb3081d2aa52cc.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(-{x}^{2})-(-2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y={x}^{2}-2.$\n\n![](images/68/683244c0b5be598da85a8875708f459d4f87319677111f1072c997d911075d68.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,({x}^{2}-2)-(-2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}((2-{x}^{2})^{2}-({x}^{2})^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4-4{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}-{x}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4-4{x}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(4x-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi ((4(1)-\\frac{4(1)^{3}}{3})-(4(-1)-\\frac{4(-1)^{3}}{3})) \\\\  & =\\pi ((4-\\frac{4}{3})-(-4+\\frac{4}{3})) \\\\  & =\\pi (8-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535941 1068 17592186535911 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535941 1069 "q-346446" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535941 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535941 1071 "The following table shows the cooling rate of a walk-in freezer over a period of $10$ hours, starting at $9\\,\\text{a.m}.$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ | $15$ | $17$ | $19$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)\\,(\\text{kilojoules/hour})$ | $5$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $k$ |\n\n If the right Riemann sum approximation of the total energy removed from $9\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $7\\,\\text{p.m.}$ has a value of $40$ kilojoules, then what is the value of $k?$ \n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535941 1072 #uuid "295aa24d-cc18-5ddf-b640-8d50f96f1e1a" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535941 1074 "The area $A$ under the curve (rate vs. time) can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{R(11)+R(13)+R(15)+R(17)+R(19)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of the rate at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $11$ | $13$ | $15$ | $17$ | $19$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(4+3+2+4+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+13) \\\\  & =2k+26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total energy removed from $9\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $7\\,\\text{p.m}.$ Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total energy removed is $40$ kilojoules, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+26 & =40 \\\\ 2k & =40-26 \\\\ k & =\\frac{14}{2} \\\\ k & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535942 1068 17592186535851 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535942 1069 "q-100832" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535942 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535942 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $-1$ and the second term is $-3.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535942 1072 #uuid "2a8b0250-b565-5cfb-a030-1477892ed03e" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535942 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe are given the first term $f(1)=-1.$ Also, the common difference of the sequence can be found as\n\n$$\nd=-3-(-1)=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-1+(n-1)(-2) \\\\  & =-1-2n+2 \\\\  & =1-2n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is $f(n)=1-2n.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535943 1068 17592186535861 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535943 1069 "q-346101" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535943 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535943 1071 "The following table shows the fuel consumption rate, $F(t),$ of a generator (in liters per hour) over a period of $8$ hours.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(hours)}$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F\\,\\text{(liters/hour)}$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ | $6$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum for $F(t)$ over the interval $[0,8]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total amount of fuel used.\n\nThe total amount of fuel is \n{{field-1}} liters." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535943 1072 #uuid "2e5afc97-cdbf-5609-994c-66b16cc595b4" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535943 1074 "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=8,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{8-0}{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{F(2)+F(4)+F(6)+F(8)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $F(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(t)$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ | $6$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+5+2+6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total amount of fuel used over a period of $8$ hours. So, the total amount of fuel is $32$ liters." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535944 1068 17592186535842 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535944 1069 "q-124592" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535944 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535944 1071 "![](images/ae/ae4ec1bfc4bf15f8ae4d79492389dd6a3fb5e3c504b0d99a43466fc08bd4b63e.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y={e}^{{x}^{2}}$ and $y={e}^{x+2}$ for $x\\in [-1,2]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535944 1072 #uuid "417976f8-e97d-5f88-9ef3-7e599b484745" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535944 1074 "Let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/74/742602117733f7e0f8fa89931ff11cdfa483c3d878a99445ec1408de6aea8f30.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y={e}^{{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={e}^{x+2}.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={e}^{x+2}.$\n\n![](images/b6/b6d2a21d75b441ca5c7c76320aad11b4dded17052056f8828e5c93b068b9bad3.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,({e}^{x+2})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{e}^{x+2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y={e}^{{x}^{2}}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1e84714cd64d30cbb4f73c93fc6dfdfeb12f10422f0524d754906709a9a26ff6.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[\\,({e}^{{x}^{2}})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{e}^{{x}^{2}}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{e}^{x+2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}{[{e}^{{x}^{2}}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[{e}^{x+2}+2]}^{2}-{[{e}^{{x}^{2}}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535945 1068 17592186535894 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535945 1069 "q-70510" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535945 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535945 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for  $f(x)={x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{x}.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535945 1072 #uuid "41b5b51f-ba4d-5684-ad06-c34aebd809fa" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535945 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{(2-1)}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{1/2-1}-(-1){x}^{(-1-1)} \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535946 1068 17592186535871 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535946 1069 "q-106414" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535946 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535946 1071 "![](images/89/8934e5b6135b97b845dad9a6b87056d2c228fd2424d3a72141df694d46ccf7c0.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535946 1072 #uuid "476be737-90ba-5bfe-8a20-c650319e8b3b" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535946 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-6,12).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-6,12),$ we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=12$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6=2pt \\\\ 12=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =2pt \\\\ -3 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(\\frac{3}{4})t,\\,y=(\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535947 1068 17592186535849 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535947 1069 "q-84735" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535947 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535947 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y=-t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535947 1072 #uuid "4d316b19-46f8-547d-95ad-bbdabb50ffb3" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535947 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-2x,\\,t=-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-2x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=-2x.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535948 1068 17592186535854 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535948 1069 "q-125795" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535948 1070 17592186045486 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535948 1071 "![](images/bf/bfcd52f31f825e6445700e8495f7acbfce46139144f8bb61e977b2971e01d877.png)\n\nThe larger polygon shown above is the image of the smaller one under the action of a dilation. Find the center of the dilation and its scale factor $k.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535948 1072 #uuid "527b9edb-ee7b-5004-b192-96e29f02e1cb" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535948 1074 "The center of a dilation lies on any line which passes through a point and its image.\n\nTherefore, we can find the center of our dilation as, for example, the point of intersection of the lines $\\overset{⟷}{B{B}^{′}}$ and $\\overset{⟷}{C{C}^{′}},$ as shown below.  \n\n![](images/d7/d7215165822a95ce9689e3666ff6ea9454048a936c372b07a6224d199b8d0ed7.png)\n\nFrom the diagram above, where we have that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}}{BC}=\\frac{6}{2}=3,\n$$\n\nand the same proportion holds for the other corresponding sides too. As a result, the absolute value of the scale factor is \n\n$$\n|k|=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, notice that our figures have different orientations, which means that the scale factor of the dilation is negative.\n\nTherefore, the scale factor is $k=-3.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535949 1068 17592186535841 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535949 1069 "q-102559" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535949 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535949 1071 "![](images/a7/a70514b3167a73ac906d0817d1dffda15617bc642bb0c1d0eaca50762b716063.png)\n\nThe figure shown above is reflected across the line ${l}_{1}$ and then this resulting figure is reflected across the line ${l}_{2}.$ Which of the following diagrams shows the final figure?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535949 1072 #uuid "5b6f1d07-c822-54bd-9a6b-61be77fdf562" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535949 1074 "First, we reflect our figure in the line ${l}_{1}:$\n\n![](images/2c/2cf56107823d9a67d27ce38bda91b815815767c8565cfce44f8a0f493127d1d8.png)\n\nNow, we reflect the resulting figure in the line ${l}_{2}:$ \n\n![](images/29/29b381def36ec661b0940d8f8f8a2f0633e983a9a70ea46bf2548a57d9f3f146.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535950 1068 17592186535905 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535950 1069 "q-335062" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535950 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535950 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535950 1072 #uuid "5cb555db-6a59-509d-947e-27c4f1a2a7e6" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535950 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535951 1068 17592186535876 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535951 1069 "q-361829" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535951 1070 17592186045486 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535951 1071 "![](images/77/778041d84c40044d6f047203a017bd64c0569bcfc9442afca9baa5160b494ae7.png)\n\nThe region shown above enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x}+1$ and $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-1.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535951 1072 #uuid "5d03566e-e00c-5038-a04e-9a446a86a195" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535951 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x}+1 & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & =x \\\\ x & =0,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=4.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7c/7ce02bca7667a9c32971b7840ff2ba4b96ef3a5c9b18aceaeb5b9b713cc7b36f.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+1.$\n\n![](images/6c/6cb557a57a189a426b6cccacd599562332b05aace5bbbfc5d996b0d53fed3359.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\sqrt{x}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\sqrt{x}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=\\frac{x}{2}+1.$\n\n![](images/26/26886afd61251cae273c086dba59dcfa912665cdb788b0c3ef866bf61907efa2.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\frac{x}{2}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{x}{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\sqrt{x}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{x}{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}({(\\sqrt{x}+2)}^{2}-{(\\frac{x}{2}+2)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x+4\\sqrt{x})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{1}{12}\\,{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}\\,{x}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\pi ((-\\frac{1}{12}\\,(4)^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,(4)^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}\\,(4)^{3/2})-(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (8) \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535952 1068 17592186535879 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535952 1068 17592186535920 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535952 1069 "q-280220" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535952 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535952 1071 "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-8,8).$ Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535952 1072 #uuid "5daa6476-7e34-5e5c-afc2-27c877b6a525" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535952 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-8,8),$ we can substitute $x=-8$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8=2pt \\\\ 8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(-\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{16}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(2)t,\\,y=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=4t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535953 1068 17592186535833 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535953 1069 "q-125558" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535953 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535953 1071 "![](images/b8/b8730b1936d1ff9f0c8d3845a70327e351b23c7085f248bcb6e99e73f0cb8c9e.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the point $(8,-4)$ under the action of a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}?$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535953 1072 #uuid "5e9dde9d-e070-5830-aa05-46ebbed13863" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535953 1074 "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ can be represented by the function  \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(\\frac{1}{2}x,\\frac{1}{2}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(8,-4),$ we have\n\n$$\n(8,-4)↦(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4))=(4,-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(4,-2).$\n\n![](images/c2/c2c00f1380ce776da3fe4e1ab2b30c969d5d7436966d907db7a0ceb5f0a13f33.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535954 1068 17592186535878 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535954 1069 "q-353938" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535954 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535954 1071 "A robotic rover moves in a straight line with velocity \n$v(t)=3{t}^{2}-t,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds and $v$ is measured in meters per second. \n\nFind the average acceleration ${a}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the rover over the time interval $t\\in [0,2].$ Do not round your answer.\n\n${a}_{\\text{avg}}=$ \n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535954 1072 #uuid "5f1c0a58-fb01-5814-8e30-7c47e655e50c" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535954 1074 "The average acceleration is the average rate of change of the velocity, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{v(b)-v(a)}{b-a} \\\\  & =\\frac{v(2)-v(0)}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{[3(2)^{2}-2]-[3(0)^{2}-0]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(12-2)-0}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average acceleration is $5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535955 1068 17592186535857 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535955 1069 "q-20315" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535955 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535955 1071 "A drone has positive velocity $v(t)$ after being flown upward from an initial height of $0$ feet at time  $t=0$ seconds. The velocity of the drone is recorded for selected values of $t$ over the interval $0\\le t\\le 40$ seconds, as shown in the table below. \n\n| $t\\,\\text{(seconds)}$ | $0$ | $7$ | $15$ | $25$ | $32$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v\\,\\text{(feet/second)}$ | $3$ | $10$ | $25$ | $40$ | $46$ | $49$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum over $[0,40]$ with five subintervals to approximate the total distance covered by the drone.\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the velocity-time graph.*" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535955 1072 #uuid "6772ccf8-308a-5e57-b20e-bbe6b51388bc" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535955 1074 "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=v(t)$ over $[0,40].$ \nIn this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the total distance can be approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈v(7)Δ{t}_{1}+v(15)Δ{t}_{2}+v(25)Δ{t}_{3}+v(32)Δ{t}_{4}+v(40)Δ{t}_{5},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $7$ | $15$ | $25$ | $32$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $7$ | $8$ | $10$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| $v(t)$ | $-$ | $10$ | $25$ | $40$ | $46$ | $49$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the number of liters and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈10\\cdot 7+25\\cdot 8+40\\cdot 10+46\\cdot 7+49\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =70+200+400+322+392 \\\\  & =1\\,384.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance covered by the drone. So, the distance is approximately $1\\,384$ feet." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535956 1068 17592186535887 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535956 1069 "q-124416" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535956 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535956 1071 "How is the angle $-\\frac{13\\pi }{18}$ normally represented in the coordinate plane?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535956 1072 #uuid "69f2d31b-6c6d-5d78-bb33-d5bfc1d325a7" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535956 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-(\\frac{13\\pi }{18})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=-{130}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle $-\\frac{13\\pi }{18}$ is equivalent to $-{130}^{∘}.$ \n\nNow, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and a vertex that lies at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/42/42c03656382d8caa36fc7937d47d31d2932e139a5282afb5e0787ea518130691.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535957 1068 17592186535863 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535957 1069 "q-125400" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535957 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535957 1071 "![](images/90/90980bb04eb7dd9906ec9a99ea3a1b7cfd9c00969bbd9b12c60fe0f80eb88840.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+2{y}^{2}=1$ and $x=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3})$ for $y\\in [-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535957 1072 #uuid "6be7f528-8a2e-5e94-a08f-bb7c33bc8c26" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535957 1074 "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equation of our first curve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2{y}^{2} & =1\\,⟹\\,x=1-2{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/16/16ca1c96786eb8e7ef12608f70d03b34b679e63d34ef41135b4fd6801bcccc27.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=1-2{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}).$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}).$\n\n![](images/27/276dea7b2d9f50e3fe171cceb64c1c7a12f84cfb18bdbe242102802ad9790f49.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\,(1)-(2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3}))\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{3})]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3})]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=1-2{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/b6/b6c6fb140bb3eaf4c49cba76af157fb46a568bc2dc24e4763facede62720f844.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[\\,(1)-(1-2{y}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{[2{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3}))}^{2}-{(2{y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi y}{3})-4{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535958 1068 17592186535874 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535958 1069 "q-100845" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535958 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535958 1071 "Find the $25$th term of the following arithmetic sequence. \n\n$$\n4,\\,8,\\,12,\\,16,\\,…\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535958 1072 #uuid "6e4f48de-276e-5a6a-aa00-d24142c4be2a" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535958 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nFirst, we will write the formula for the $n$th term of this sequence. From the first few terms\n\n$$\n4,\\,8,\\,12,\\,16,\\,…,\n$$\n\nwe see that the first term is ${a}_{1}=4$ and the common difference is \n\n$$\nd=8-4=4.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =4+(n-1)(4) \\\\  & =4+4n-4 \\\\  & =4n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use our formula to find the $25$th term. All we have to do is substitute $n=25:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =4n \\\\ {a}_{25} & =4(25) \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $25$th term of the sequence is ${a}_{25}=100.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535959 1068 17592186535885 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535959 1069 "q-110665" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535959 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535959 1071 "The table below defines the function $f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is an even function, find the value of $a+b.$\n\n| **$x$** | $-90$ | $-65$ | $-40$ | $40$ | $65$ | $90$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $16$ | $b-2$ | $-8$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $a+6$ |" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535959 1072 #uuid "71f2cfbf-31cb-5074-ba4b-cb4c0df22324" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535959 1074 "Since $f(x)$ is an even function, we must have that $f(-x)=f(x).$ \n\nUsing the fact that $f(-90)=f(90),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-90) & =f(90) \\\\ 16 & =a+6 \\\\ a & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $f(-65)=f(65),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-65) & =f(65) \\\\ b-2 & =-1 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=10+1=11.\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535960 1068 17592186535865 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535960 1069 "q-115745" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535960 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535960 1071 "The following table shows the rate $r(t)$, in flour bags per minute, at which a machine makes flour over a period of $75$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $15$ | $30$ | $45$ | $60$ | $75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r\\,\\text{(flour bags/minute)}$ | $30$ | $25$ | $35$ | $40$ | $35$ | $25$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum for $r(t)$ over the interval $[0,75]$, with five subintervals, to approximate the number of flour bags made by the machine in $75$ minutes." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535960 1072 #uuid "7984804e-ee09-5ab8-9ea3-cfb9bf84394d" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535960 1074 "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=75,$ and there are $n=5$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{75-0}{5}=15.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{r(15)+r(30)+r(45)+r(60)+r(75)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $r(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $15$ | $30$ | $45$ | $60$ | $75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)$ | $25$ | $35$ | $40$ | $35$ | $25$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+35+40+35+25)\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =160\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =2\\,400\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of flour bags made by the machine in $75$ minutes.  So, the number of flour bags is approximately $2\\,400.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535961 1068 17592186535902 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535961 1069 "q-34483" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535961 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535961 1071 "How is the angle $-\\frac{17\\pi }{9}$ normally represented in the coordinate plane?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535961 1072 #uuid "799cf069-368d-5fad-8ff3-f06fd1cc93a6" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535961 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-(\\frac{17\\pi }{9})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=-{340}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle $-\\frac{17\\pi }{9}$ is equivalent to $-{340}^{∘}.$ \n\nNow, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and a vertex that lies at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/a8/a82ca9d59a88071320c95a78d10900c73c32e4c156dddb32a57603121923b8c5.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535962 1068 17592186535895 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535962 1069 "q-346462" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535962 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535962 1071 "The table below gives the medication infusion rate $r(t)$, in milligrams per minute, for a patient.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $13$ | $18$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)\\,\\text{(mg/min)}$ | $6$ | $5$ | $3$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum over $[0,18]$ with five subintervals to approximate the total medication delivered (in milligrams).\n\nThe total medication delivered is\n{{field-1}} milligrams." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535962 1072 #uuid "7b623f34-55bd-5cf9-90b7-bc3a51b954e0" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535962 1074 "Let $A$ denote the area under the curve $y=r(t)$ over $[0,18].$ In this case, we have $n=5$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is computed as \n\n$$\nA≈r(4)Δ{t}_{1}+r(7)Δ{t}_{2}+r(10)Δ{t}_{3}+r(13)Δ{t}_{4}+r(18)Δ{t}_{5},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i}-{t}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $13$ | $18$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $-$ | $4$ | $3$ | $3$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| $r(t)$ | $-$ | $5$ | $3$ | $4$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nWe plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈5\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =20+9+12+6+5 \\\\  & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total accumulation of medication. So, the total medication delivered is $52$ milligrams." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535963 1068 17592186535877 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535963 1069 "q-86344" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535963 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535963 1071 "The following table gives the speed of a minivan measured during a $4$-second interval.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{seconds})$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)\\,(\\text{meters/second})$ | $17$ | $18$ | $k$ | $25$ | $27$ |\n\n If the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance that the minivan traveled during the $4$-second interval has a value of $85$ meters, what is the value of $k?$\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the speed-time graph.*" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535963 1072 #uuid "86b94149-e5f8-5f16-b1b9-921b18ee69eb" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535963 1074 "The area $A$ under the curve $y=v(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{v(1)+v(2)+v(3)+v(4)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $v(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)$ | $18$ | $k$ | $25$ | $27$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(18+k+25+27)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =70+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance that the minivan traveled. Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance is $85$ meters,  we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70+k & =85 \\\\ k & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535964 1068 17592186535896 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535964 1068 17592186535915 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535964 1069 "q-280225" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535964 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535964 1071 "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=-24x.$ Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535964 1072 #uuid "8855f85e-af22-5017-b471-837c5222e69b" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535964 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-24x \\\\ 4p & =-24 \\\\ p & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-6){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-6)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-6{t}^{2},\\,y=-12t.\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535965 1068 17592186535908 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535965 1069 "q-125555" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535965 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535965 1071 "![](images/ac/ac7f02919896aeb40859b1d78608ec7620c26ea77becc806a28d39ef8abacd12.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ and $x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ for $y\\in [1,2]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=3.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535965 1072 #uuid "88a251ee-6143-5e86-bf44-46f8b33042cc" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535965 1074 "First, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/76/76196a70cdbc65d2f91b4ba500ea83083be5cfe10fe3f3bd09c035e3ea7bcfba.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as a difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\n![](images/e5/e5d8affefbde216fed0bf6acd7d464e921c1cf04e2578050d12c928d6dee5bd0.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(3)-(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[3-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4002c7922eac8e7c97d402833eabc3621ed4da2d13799d07206a45cf0afd1f1.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(3)-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[3-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{[3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({[3-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}-{[3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535966 1068 17592186535880 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535966 1069 "q-70507" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535966 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535966 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for  $f(x)={x}^{8}-{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535966 1072 #uuid "8a7a18a3-9c4b-5644-bbba-c016ec6ec070" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535966 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8}-{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =8{x}^{(8-1)}-3{x}^{(3-1)}+(-2){x}^{-2-1} \\\\  & =8{x}^{7}-3{x}^{2}-2{x}^{-3} \\\\  & =8{x}^{7}-3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535967 1068 17592186535855 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535967 1069 "q-346443" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535967 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535967 1071 "The following table shows the water drainage rate of a reservoir over a period of $8$ hours, starting at $2\\,\\text{p.m}.$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)\\,(\\text{gallons/hour})$ | $2$ | $k$ | $5$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nIf the right Riemann sum approximation of the total water drained from $2\\,\\text{p.m}$ to $10\\,\\text{p.m.}$ has a value of $30$ gallons, then what is the value of $k?$ \n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535967 1072 #uuid "8de05636-df00-52b0-a8f6-5c5d3db73b2e" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535967 1074 "The area $A$ under the curve (rate vs. time) can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{R(4)+R(6)+R(8)+R(10)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of the rate at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $k$ | $5$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+5+4+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+12) \\\\  & =2k+24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total water drained from $2\\,\\text{p.m}$ to $10\\,\\text{p.m}.$ Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total drainage is $30$ gallons, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+24 & =30 \\\\ 2k & =30-24 \\\\ k & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535968 1068 17592186535837 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535968 1069 "q-119167" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535968 1070 17592186045486 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535968 1071 "The volume of a sphere decreases at a rate that's proportional to its surface area. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $k>0.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535968 1072 #uuid "907d5b64-98c1-552d-a43d-a699db06e688" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535968 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*Its **volume decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **surface area**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its surface area $S.$* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kS,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$ Now, using the fact that $S=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\pi k{r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{2}}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535969 1068 17592186535846 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535969 1069 "q-30045" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535969 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535969 1071 "![](images/43/43cff74165732892223841f6780e7aa22c024e3b3c173e60ce15d8d6841a5a08.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-1$ and $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x$ for $x\\in [-2,\\frac{1}{2}]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=5.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535969 1072 #uuid "923c9707-06d1-5031-b8df-221476502c14" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535969 1074 "Let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7a/7aad98fe0513514aa8827bea4e9ffbac5b70bcb8da7923effd789988c81e286f.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{2}-1.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{2}-1.$\n\n![](images/62/623b59b7aca8b33c6149ac6b0916d1eeb425adc3f74568868383806cd9b03b12.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}[\\,(5)-({x}^{2}-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x.$\n\n![](images/c0/c0467a3bf02287589f897586a1358e4abb02759134b6b58622125319dc7331da.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[\\,(5)-(-\\frac{3}{2}x)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1/2}({[6-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[5+\\frac{3}{2}x]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535970 1068 17592186535888 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535970 1069 "q-348267" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535970 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535970 1071 "![](images/fc/fc70e0f7c7ac82e399e520faa39cf04d252501da46834e88c06d8eba2b8d192d.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x+{y}^{2}=16$ and $x+4y=16$ for $y\\in [0,4]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=16.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535970 1072 #uuid "92412be4-49e1-54ee-8609-445cb6da4a1d" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535970 1074 "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2} & =16\\,⟹\\,x=16-{y}^{2} \\\\ x+4y & =16\\,⟹\\,x=16-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f3/f39f9f399ea4d0e65a499f34d41158ddbaf5319a02e057e097b4f80c8197ba9e.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=16-{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=16-4y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=16-4y.$\n\n![](images/47/47d985510b37b7a14e82dfc20bc136694e7ba2e17172b35be45ebde88d238677.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\,(16)-(16-4y)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[4y]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=16-{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e5494005010f3cfb8fb8b116c4b8ca91e8d3bf2712ad45c60f0766a2021b19a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(16)-(16-{y}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[{y}^{2}]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}({(4y)}^{2}-{({y}^{2})}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(16{y}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{16{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{16(4)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(4)^{5}}{5})-0] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1024}{3}-\\frac{1024}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2048\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535971 1068 17592186535917 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535971 1069 "q-106443" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535971 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535971 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=-\\frac{1}{6}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535971 1072 #uuid "9466ac77-f4bf-5bd1-8cef-81367e73b3b6" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535971 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-4x,\\,t=-6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =36{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-4x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{y}^{2} & =-4x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{9}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}x.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535972 1068 17592186535847 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535972 1069 "q-354500" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535972 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535972 1071 "A particle’s acceleration in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is given by the function $a(t)=\\sqrt{t},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds.  \n\nFind the average acceleration ${a}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the particle on the interval $[1,4].$ Do not round your answer.\n\n${a}_{\\text{avg}}=$ \n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535972 1072 #uuid "948c8637-9616-53cf-93bb-0432892cbe59" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535972 1074 "To calculate the average value of the acceleration on $[{t}_{1},{t}_{2}],$ we use the average value formula\n\n$$\n{a}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{4-1}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2})|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 8)-(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{14}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{9}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average acceleration is $\\frac{14}{9}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535973 1068 17592186535840 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535973 1069 "q-70466" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535973 1070 17592186045486 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535973 1071 "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(t)=6{t}^{6}+15{t}^{5}+6{t}^{4}.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535973 1072 #uuid "9b05fed3-4ab6-51d7-8e10-a90e6b0e2462" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535973 1074 "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(t)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n6{t}^{6}+15{t}^{5}+6{t}^{4}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $3{t}^{4}.$ We can factor out the $3{t}^{4}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{4}(2{t}^{2}+5t+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{t}^{2}+5t+2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{4}(2{t}^{2}+5t+2) & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{4}(2t+1)(t+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $t=0,$ $t=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and $t=-2.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535974 1068 17592186535914 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535974 1069 "q-80488" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535974 1070 17592186045486 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535974 1071 "The area of a square increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to its perimeter. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535974 1072 #uuid "9d65b7da-354a-5fe6-8886-5852de4be621" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535974 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a square **increases** at a **rate** that's **inversely proportional** to its **perimeter**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a square* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *inversely proportional* to its *perimeter.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{4s}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a square is given by \n\n$$\nA={s}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{4s} \\\\ 2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{4s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{(\\frac{k}{4s})}{2s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{8{s}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{8}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{s}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535975 1068 17592186535864 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535975 1069 "q-74337" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535975 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535975 1071 "Estimate the area under the curve $y=2x+3$ over the interval $[-1,1]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535975 1072 #uuid "9e633987-af32-5b5c-bd64-b66c42ee580f" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535975 1074 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/be/be5f6207531c18acecdb6a7ba3890e23913de4a78546db620f2986dd0467fa6d.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-0.5)+f(0)+f(0.5)+f(1)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(2+3+4+5)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =14\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[-1,1],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535976 1068 17592186535860 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535976 1069 "q-30047" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535976 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535976 1071 "![](images/cf/cf2c500974f9c88751b9dec98cc53b5d3616b736f3ab0504adae5879cac2001a.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y=2{x}^{2}-2x$ and $y=3-3{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [-\\frac{3}{5},1]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=5.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535976 1072 #uuid "a2e19710-89b7-5129-86a4-6f0be5f110ca" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535976 1074 "Let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/d5/d5593e8c1e1d544acb8824d9428823d3d6cef3971a1cedd4b087a7902b0e2239.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=3-3{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=2{x}^{2}-2x.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=2{x}^{2}-2x.$\n\n![](images/bd/bddc78f683e28ec6e97fddb32f95dfdaaff27d3aafbdfc61f424857916f81a6c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(5)-(2{x}^{2}-2x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=3-3{x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/cc/cc049c46593baf1d96c04b95680d6b444ab9ceffc64d1f23f310f70e78684c14.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}[\\,(5)-(3-3{x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3/5}^{1}({[5-2{x}^{2}+2x]}^{2}-{[2+3{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535977 1068 17592186535886 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535977 1069 "q-125546" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535977 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535977 1071 "![](images/55/559548dc5f2570d1341760ab87a2619646ed6985fd6437cb29442dc58bc6fee4.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x={y}^{2}+2y-1$ and $x=-1-{y}^{2}$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535977 1072 #uuid "a2f8487e-855b-5e52-8eac-210ae0123755" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535977 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y-1 & =-1-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+2y & =0 \\\\ 2y(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=0.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/8f/8f1bccafe64a6926bdb214410453cecafa7059f74a539b155a4a4e6cccd67d1a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x={y}^{2}+2y-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=-1-{y}^{2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=-1-{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d1d1ed82fadf105997e00d39b99992cdeba1d41be13cf599db63b34902e149a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[\\,(-1-{y}^{2})-(-2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[1-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x={y}^{2}+2y-1.$\n\n![](images/04/040e0a87b4bb5c7121a27fd2cc94c520f76919afbb86a49cfed2ed0cf57c1561.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[\\,({y}^{2}+2y-1)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[{y}^{2}+2y-1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[{y}^{2}+2y+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[1-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[{y}^{2}+2y+1]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({[1-{y}^{2}]}^{2}-{[(y+1)^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({(1-{y}^{2})}^{2}-(y+1)^{4})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535978 1068 17592186535881 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535978 1069 "q-100834" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535978 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535978 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the first term is $-5$ and the common difference is $-1.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535978 1072 #uuid "a3ce8ce6-6748-574c-a1ce-f27315ba87e1" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535978 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the first term is $f(1)=-5$ and the common difference is $d=-1.$ So, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-5+(n-1)(-1) \\\\  & =-5-n+1 \\\\  & =-4-n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is $f(n)=-4-n.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535979 1068 17592186535856 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535979 1069 "q-10292" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535979 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535979 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=2{x}^{7}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+2x-2.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535979 1072 #uuid "a746a92c-9180-5bad-bfc6-5ca557baf9cb" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535979 1074 "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{7}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+2x-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2})+2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 7{x}^{(7-1)}+(-2){x}^{(-2-1)}+2\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =14{x}^{6}-2{x}^{-3}+2 \\\\  & =14{x}^{6}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535980 1068 17592186535893 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535980 1069 "q-125692" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535980 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535980 1071 "![](images/98/98e64a245737736bfc987fe9230b1ee3fc81eef4aedfea8c2e5e9bcf949f8203.png)\n\nWhat is the image of the polygon shown above under a dilation that is centered at the origin and has a scale factor of $-\\frac{2}{3}?$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535980 1072 #uuid "a76fa1c3-fa48-5812-bf8b-fa26865dd75b" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535980 1074 "The dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $-\\frac{2}{3}$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(-\\frac{2}{3}x,-\\frac{2}{3}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the vertices $(-3,-3),$ $(0,0),$  and $(-3,3),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3,-3) & ↦(-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3),\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3))=(2,2) \\\\ (0,0) & ↦(-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0,\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 0)=(0,0) \\\\ (-3,3) & ↦(-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3),\\,-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3)=(2,-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following polygon.\n\n![](images/29/2921272ffd1c38c082bf2123086d8c7b19a910c91d132810042b86728b820f8d.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535981 1068 17592186535834 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535981 1068 17592186535904 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535981 1068 17592186535919 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535981 1069 "q-335403" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535981 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535981 1071 "Consider the inhomogeneous system  ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $y(t)={e}^{-At}x(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-At}g(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $y(t)-y(0)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $y(0)=x(0)$ \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $y(t)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${e}^{At}y(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${e}^{At}y(t)=x(t)$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by  \n{{field-3}}." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535981 1072 #uuid "ace7005c-d6c8-5219-91cc-32f47feb4972" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535981 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Differentiating $y(t)={e}^{-At}x(t)$ and applying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{-At})^{′}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-A){e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =-A{e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{differentiating and using the product rule}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ Evaluating $y(t)={e}^{-At}x(t)$ at $t=0$ gives\n\n$$\ny(0)={e}^{-A\\cdot 0}x(0)=Ix(0)=x(0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{evaluating at }t=0.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ Multiplying both sides of\n\n$$\ny(t)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,ds\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L6}$ by ${e}^{At}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}y(t) & ={e}^{At}x(0)+{e}^{At}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L6}$ by $\\text{multiplying both sides by }{e}^{At}.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535982 1068 17592186535907 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535982 1069 "q-74350" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535982 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535982 1071 "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[2,6].$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $8$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[2,6].$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535982 1072 #uuid "ae6e559e-361b-5b37-89a9-1d035fa07b1f" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535982 1074 "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈f(3.5)Δ{x}_{1}+f(4)Δ{x}_{2}+f(6)Δ{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $-$ | $1.5$ | $0.5$ | $2$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈2\\cdot 1.5+3.5\\cdot 0.5+8\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3+1.75+16 \\\\  & =20.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535983 1068 17592186535890 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535983 1069 "q-350914" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535983 1070 17592186045486 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535983 1071 "![](images/80/8063ba9fbe4c569519eea27e5a5cd69a8f0e708b03d153942fbf22e9345af96a.png)\n\nThe region above enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}+2$ and $y=20-{x}^{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=20.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535983 1072 #uuid "b034c5b9-fc9c-52d3-a856-d07f183d8ea8" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535983 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2 & =20-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=-3$ to $x=3.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f1/f196ef4a3b29a9d62609b0a9290f34d2cb1ef1351baa4883441fd89e9345e4ed.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=20-{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{2}+2.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{2}+2.$\n\n![](images/c0/c006e66d82e0059b341520bebe983fa75ed144e3050ca8c4493a4b6df3e8f352.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(20)-({x}^{2}+2)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[18-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=20-{x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/c9/c91a8e5ed0881ed0c8f34fa2ed6ccb5f95ff75a2f3b48e2d340f8a8856885e3a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}[\\,(20)-(20-{x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[18-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{[{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}((18-{x}^{2})^{2}-({x}^{2})^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}(324-36{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}-{x}^{4})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-3}^{3}(324-36{x}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(324x-12{x}^{3})|}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =\\pi ((324(3)-12(3)^{3})-(324(-3)-12(-3)^{3})) \\\\  & =\\pi ((972-324)-(-972+324)) \\\\  & =\\pi (648-(-648)) \\\\  & =1296\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535984 1068 17592186535844 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535984 1069 "q-124601" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535984 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535984 1071 "![](images/dc/dccfcb991a6c302c522ea73381544d2e6f93b7310f54040151a00832473596b8.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y=8x$ and $y=-{x}^{4}$ for $x\\in [-2,0]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=1.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535984 1072 #uuid "b5e827a7-4590-5f6c-9029-38608edd38d6" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535984 1074 "Let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/33/335b300d66c7a51a37d595f5fb99a281c00ec6a5be62e1be12c2a3104cd54694.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=-{x}^{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y=8x.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=8x.$\n\n![](images/aa/aae56ea01faa2c09f004fe2506c036f4d5e5b2e591e2e653f4794e4a3cecc309.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}[\\,(1)-(8x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[1-8x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=-{x}^{4}.$\n\n![](images/63/635d3aabdc16fd90c2e2a06a042f98abe5dc16f1d7747394cd9083858023adee.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}[\\,(1)-(-{x}^{4})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[1+{x}^{4}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[1-8x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{[1+{x}^{4}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}({[1-8x]}^{2}-{[1+{x}^{4}]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535985 1068 17592186535892 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535985 1069 "q-49115" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535985 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535985 1071 "A right Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-2$ and $x=1$ for each of the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *underestimate* of the area?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535985 1072 #uuid "b7189171-aff4-5525-9ffc-edd7b878fae4" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535985 1074 "The right Riemann sum underestimates the area under the respective curve if the function is decreasing over the given interval. \n\nOf the given options, the only function that is decreasing over $x\\in [-2,1]$ is as follows.\n\n![](images/12/1287968881b822c475474e0a1e89ed589473d24f7cdf278c6d9fbd18b960344b.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535986 1068 17592186535906 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535986 1069 "q-102581" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535986 1070 17592186045486 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535986 1071 "![](images/69/691ef4d815f6150b5940c323c5a2be09dc0f0ea1abdd3be36560d931fd1f5015.png)\n\nThe polygon $T$ is mapped to the polygon ${T}^{′}$ under the action of a reflection. What is the equation of the line of reflection?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535986 1072 #uuid "b9f087f3-735f-5a05-b67f-a26f82bae379" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535986 1074 "The line of reflection is the perpendicular bisector of the segments joining each point to its image. \n\nTherefore, the equation of the line of reflection is $y=x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d1/d1278df44d05d0bb2169e72c1dce84650b0d4f269dd14d57c4d046f634e1ab7a.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535987 1068 17592186535853 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535987 1069 "q-84714" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535987 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535987 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=-10x?$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535987 1072 #uuid "bf998376-0b5d-5f0e-ac72-6bbc0864becd" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535987 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-10x \\\\ 4p & =-10 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-\\frac{5}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-\\frac{5}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y=-5t.\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535988 1068 17592186535848 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535988 1069 "q-30046" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535988 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535988 1071 "![](images/c2/c2a8da9dd6d088d30b0b023d50ebf2c23e2bcfda89cd390ec16779694ae5eb39.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=x$ in the first quadrant is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535988 1072 #uuid "bfc9867e-24b3-5baa-adb5-18feba56d583" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535988 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =x \\\\ {x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/82/82783afd970ec3f0e90a8b97a12207b0ed8cbf82323e0512e9fe36b99a6fdc2f.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{3}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{3}.$\n\n![](images/4a/4a9a72a194226f73da234e03b93e4b255849315808ac948c0958d8dee52b4f73.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(2)-({x}^{3})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2-{x}^{3}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=x.$\n\n![](images/4c/4c53b4b1ad169119cadf490636b12235b3fc225a08d57c11d4f8e26318797f25.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(2)-(x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2-x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2-{x}^{3}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2-x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2-{x}^{3}]}^{2}-{[2-x]}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535989 1068 17592186535883 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535989 1069 "q-354478" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535989 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535989 1071 "A small rover moves along a straight track with velocity \n$v(t)=\\sqrt{t}+2,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds and $v$ is measured in meters per second.\n\nFind the average velocity ${v}_{\\text{avg}}$ on the interval $[1,4].$ Do not round your answer.\n\n${v}_{\\text{avg}}=$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535989 1072 #uuid "c00bebe5-d517-59a2-a7ee-fce25fbbc516" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535989 1074 "To calculate the average value of the velocity on $[{t}_{1},{t}_{2}],$ we use the average value formula\n\n$$\n{v}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}}{\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{4-1}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\sqrt{t}+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}+2t)|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 8+8)-(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\frac{16}{3}+8)-(\\frac{2}{3}+2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{40}{3}-\\frac{8}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{32}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{9}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the average velocity is $\\frac{32}{9}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535990 1068 17592186535916 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535990 1069 "q-124391" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535990 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535990 1071 "Which of the following diagrams shows the unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $-\\frac{5\\pi }{9}$ in the standard position?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535990 1072 #uuid "c3b96603-bf9c-5b42-9877-9f4fe08d9aee" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535990 1074 "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{5\\pi }{9})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=-{100}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $-\\frac{5\\pi }{9}$ is equivalent to $-{100}^{∘}.$\n\nThen, recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/10/10e0ca8ea02ea6daefa4ebcd91cb1fc6d9264e065a7db26eee9a8bfdeb38e4f3.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535991 1068 17592186535875 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535991 1069 "q-18697" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535991 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535991 1071 "Let $(x,y)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $x-y?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.04x-0.08y-4=0 \\\\ 2x-3y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535991 1072 #uuid "c4ed7da6-2468-5aec-962f-cfbcbb62e8fe" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535991 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the top equation by $100,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.04x-0.08y-4=0 & |\\,\\times 100 \\\\ 2x-3y=4 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-8y-400=0 \\\\ 2x-3y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ we will get a $-4x$ term in the second equation that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-8y-400=0 &  \\\\ 2x-3y=4 & |\\,\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-8y-400=0 \\\\ -4x+6y=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{4x}-8y-400 & =0 \\\\ -\\cancel{4x}+6y & =-8 \\\\ (-8y+6y)-400 & =-8 \\\\ -2y & =392 \\\\ y & =-196\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=-196$ into the equation $2x-3y=4$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y & =4 \\\\ 2x-3(-196) & =4 \\\\ 2x+588 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =-584 \\\\ x & =-292\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-292$ and $y=-196.$ So, \n\n$$\nx-y=-292-(-196)=-292+196=-96.\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535992 1068 17592186535845 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535992 1069 "q-125574" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535992 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535992 1071 "![](images/84/841dfcfabb27fe93002cb05c8d84ad9da2e0a9f6b810476e7964651e515e757c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the point $(8,-6)$ under the action of a dilation with center $O$ and scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}?$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535992 1072 #uuid "cac69546-49cc-507e-802a-92a175e045ac" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535992 1074 "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(\\frac{1}{2}x,\\frac{1}{2}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $(8,-6),$ we have\n\n$$\n(8,-6)↦(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-6))=(4,-3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(4,-3).$\n\n![](images/53/53df2214415b81380c96af98b2d073e2a16480715386926603cd471eec66dec6.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535993 1068 17592186535859 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535993 1069 "q-134213" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535993 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535993 1071 "| **Price per Bag, in $$$ ($x$)** | $2$ | $3$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Number of Bags Sold ($y$)** | $28$ | $20$ | $24$ | $16$ |\n\nThe table above shows the results of a survey conducted in four fast food outlets. It shows data on the number of bags of onion rings sold daily and the price per bag. Calculate the covariance of the data sets." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535993 1072 #uuid "cd62ff9e-af99-5108-83c4-43b98934fd5b" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535993 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (2+3+3+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (28+20+24+16) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 88 \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2\\,-\\,3)(28\\,-\\,22)+(3\\,-\\,3)(20\\,-\\,22)+(3\\,-\\,3)(24\\,-\\,22)+(4\\,-\\,3)(16\\,-\\,22)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-1)(6)+(0)(-2)+(0)(2)+(1)(-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-6+0+0+(-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535994 1068 17592186535901 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535994 1069 "q-1861" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535994 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535994 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=-4+2{x}^{7}-12{x}^{2}+{x}^{5/4}.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535994 1072 #uuid "cd92c8db-d853-53b3-99d2-6ad9bcebe052" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535994 1074 "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4+2{x}^{7}-12{x}^{2}+{x}^{5/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{7})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4)+2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7})-12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/4}) \\\\  & =0+2\\cdot 7{x}^{(7-1)}-12\\cdot 2{x}^{(2-1)}+\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {x}^{(\\frac{5}{4}-1)} \\\\  & =14{x}^{6}-24x+\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{1/4} \\\\  & =14{x}^{6}-24x+\\frac{5\\sqrt[4]{x}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535995 1068 17592186535899 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535995 1069 "q-49092" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535995 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535995 1071 "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[0,15]$ with a regular step size. If the right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $60$, then what must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $k$ |" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535995 1072 #uuid "d36beb24-ba8a-5d0f-80e1-d03ab3463ddd" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535995 1074 "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(5)+f(10)+f(15)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+4+k)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(7+k)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5k+35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A$ must be  $60,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k+35 & =60 \\\\ 5k & =60-35 \\\\ k & =\\frac{25}{5} \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535996 1068 17592186535850 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535996 1069 "q-361808" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535996 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535996 1071 "![](images/5a/5a242a5bec4be4cb58b77289ea2ccf49bf22a368c19ec18f10790a417bfbff72.png)\n\nThe region shown above enclosed by the curves $y=3-x$ and $y={x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [0,1]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=4.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535996 1072 #uuid "d651054f-0a78-573d-a5b9-95d84b459c37" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535996 1074 "First, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/25/255abcfb1a9034df297dc4939d945d8c3b1c4bd717cebde2844aa2a229fd5258.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=3-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/4b/4b480a3bb7312a59edcabb133100bee1ebd5777664ea490b58744171e05e3083.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(4)-({x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=3-x.$\n\n![](images/20/20de93738463a5b04c0c5c0884d1db7d8867503931122c99a27fc82493b64edb.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper})-(\\text{lower})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(4)-(3-x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4-{x}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[1+x]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({(4-{x}^{2})}^{2}-{(1+x)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-9{x}^{2}-2x+15)\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}-3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+15x)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{1}{5}(1)^{5}-3(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}+15(1))-0] \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}-3-1+15) \\\\  & =\\pi (11+\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{55}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{56\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535997 1068 17592186535910 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535997 1069 "q-88115" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535997 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535997 1071 "![](images/3a/3a512ef7399ac46c611335a9f957bfae9a14e802ddd39e492f6184e0e149fb7e.png)\n\nWhat is the image of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under a dilation that is centered at the origin and has a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}?$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535997 1072 #uuid "d78079e7-2f05-54ed-bf64-8723ddffd37e" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535997 1074 "A dilation with center at the origin and a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(\\frac{1}{3}x,\\frac{1}{3}y).\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the endpoints $A(-6,-3)$ and $B(3,6),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6,-3) & ↦(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6),\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot -3)=(-2,-1) \\\\ (3,6) & ↦(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3),\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6)=(1,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting points are ${A}^{′}(-2,-1)$ and ${B}^{′}(1,2).$\n\nFinally, we connect ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ using a segment, as shown below. \n\n![](images/ba/ba27f325ca104397544c7b5d19078d59cf63209a83d89de73cdeb752f5c0fc84.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535998 1068 17592186535869 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535998 1069 "q-86194" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535998 1070 17592186045486 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535998 1071 "![](images/6b/6ba207cd34725ec2e3995eb0f90e63bedc8573300335b2d31163457ef8ee9bad.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x={y}^{2}+1$ and $x=2{y}^{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-2.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535998 1072 #uuid "db9dcd58-6ee1-5525-a1ac-d82114a9c0f2" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535998 1074 "First, to determine the limits of integration, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =-1,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=1.$\n\nNext, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/41/41f8dff0676c5cf8952f46b174ff4f98deeb97eec272e417f145b4782770c156.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=2{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x={y}^{2}+1.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={y}^{2}+1.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4d3d75dfab4aa6382bf47f06a57036ec612a05c90d0764cd48fdc1f150f2453.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\,({y}^{2}+1)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[{y}^{2}+3]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=2{y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/6f/6f79c95e2b2d3aaf71e6dfac5ca93fa20261f5f40e1660cefc4ac23af3e56cf8.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[\\,(2{y}^{2})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2{y}^{2}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[{y}^{2}+3]}^{2}-{[2{y}^{2}+2]}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-3{y}^{4}-2{y}^{2}+5)\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{3{y}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}+5y)|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(-\\frac{3\\cdot {1}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{3}}{3}+5\\cdot 1)-(-\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)^{3}}{3}+5\\cdot (-1))] \\\\  & =\\frac{112\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535999 1068 17592186535866 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535999 1069 "q-149613" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535999 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535999 1071 "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{2,\\,14,\\,18,\\,6\\},\\,y=\\{-2,\\,-1,\\,-3,\\,2\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=10$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=-1.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535999 1072 #uuid "dbf7f381-31f1-5a31-a806-20d912ae1de5" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535999 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2-10)(-2-(-1))+(14-10)(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,+(18-10)(-3-(-1))+(6-10)(2-(-1))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-8)(-1)+(4)(0)+(8)(-2)+(-4)(3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[8+0-16-12] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-20) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536000 1068 17592186535889 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536000 1069 "q-18855" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536000 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536000 1071 "Let $(x,y)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $x+y?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.05x-0.03y=0.04 \\\\ 0.4x+0.2y=0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536000 1072 #uuid "dd60924f-af00-5e97-9e77-c29eb2bc8035" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536000 1074 "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the first equations by $100$ and the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.05x-0.03y=0.04 & |\\times 100 \\\\ 0.4x+0.2y=0.1 & |\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y=4 \\\\ 4x+2y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $3,$ we will get a $-6y$ term in the first equation that can cancel with the $6y$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y=4 & |\\,\\times (2) \\\\ 4x+2y=1 & |\\,\\times (3)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}10x-6y=8 \\\\ 12x+6y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-\\cancel{6y} & =8 \\\\ 12x+\\cancel{6y} & =3 \\\\ (10x+12x) & =(8+3) \\\\ 22x & =11 \\\\ x & =\\frac{11}{22} \\\\ x & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=0.5$ into the equation $4x+2y=1$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2y & =1 \\\\ 4(0.5)+2y & =1 \\\\ 2+2y & =1 \\\\ 2y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=0.5$ and $y=-0.5.$ So, \n\n$$\nx+y=0.5+(-0.5)=0.\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536001 1068 17592186535891 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536001 1069 "q-49945" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536001 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536001 1071 "![](images/1c/1c22369a6f1abc89437de1977135208ef5d0e762a4971df1c9ace14b6af932cd.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{5y}{8}$ and $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=-2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536001 1072 #uuid "dd731e5d-ee05-5b06-811e-7a9b6ae9709a" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536001 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{5y}{8} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =5y \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-5y & =0 \\\\ y(2y-5) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/50/504e204d73a1f87d72a321e7c0f58eadf01840ff3b8dbc5214f08e50fcbdcf9b.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{8}y.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=\\frac{5}{8}y.$\n\n![](images/0c/0c43a01a8548112d8dc1f30f9059c560ac7bec1e679a6c76394d83e4bfbea668.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\,(\\frac{5}{8}y)-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{5}{8}y+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}.$\n\n![](images/d8/d8478ef13983e6529fa10147f9659171e31a5555cb799ec1fad13d1fe8b25191.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\,(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4})-(-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{5}{8}y+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{5/2}({[\\frac{5}{8}y+2]}^{2}-{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536002 1068 17592186535909 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536002 1069 "q-124662" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536002 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536002 1071 "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(1,1)$ to the point $N(3,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy=$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536002 1072 #uuid "e6367608-0b6c-56a6-854c-a12592ac7a14" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536002 1074 "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+j$ and $b=3\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+j+t(3\\,i+3\\,j-(i+j)) \\\\  & =i+j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t+1$ and $y(t)=2t+1$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{2t+1}\\cdot 2+\\frac{1}{(2t+1)^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)-\\frac{1}{2t+1}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot 1+1)-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1+1}]-[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot 0+1)-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 0+1}] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536003 1068 17592186535900 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536003 1069 "q-115700" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536003 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536003 1071 "A right Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-2$ and $x=0$ for each of the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *underestimate* of the area?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536003 1072 #uuid "f185512a-6bdc-56ba-b826-59b4b54f5782" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536003 1074 "The right Riemann sum underestimates the area under the respective curve if the function is decreasing over the given interval. \n\nOf the given options, the only function that is decreasing over $x\\in [-2,0]$ is as follows.\n\n![](images/54/54da2c81b258a8b4768ff8550e261c1f8997c64df40e83160e674d9d223c601e.png)" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536004 1068 17592186535903 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536004 1069 "q-72703" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536004 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536004 1071 "![](images/d4/d4cf4f9e1cc145154e5f542a5064b51d6385e7f81d12c7187dff436f5e7e10f9.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be graphs of odd functions?" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536004 1072 #uuid "f2ca34f0-80b3-509b-af1e-131cf84ebf9f" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536004 1074 "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n- Graphs II and III do not have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. So, they are **not** odd functions.\n\n- On the other hand, graphs I and IV do have rotational symmetry about the origin. If we rotate the graphs ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same pictures. This means that graphs I and IV show odd functions.\n\n![](images/60/60a38abf4723f137ef0f824613ab15282c9ff5e3098fac13173630baef577e45.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536005 1068 17592186535870 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536005 1069 "q-86372" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536005 1070 17592186045485 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536005 1071 "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(t)$ on the closed interval $[10,40].$ The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(t)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $480.$ What must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $t$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(t)$ | $18$ | $12$ | $k$ | $16$ |" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536005 1072 #uuid "fbb4c94f-2803-5b7e-b57a-776b92b59056" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536005 1074 "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(20)+f(30)+f(40)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(t)$ | $12$ | $k$ | $16$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(12+k+16)\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =(28+k)\\cdot 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $A$ must be $480$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(28+k)\\cdot 10 & =480 \\\\ 28+k & =48 \\\\ k & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536006 1068 17592186535835 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536006 1069 "q-85687" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536006 1070 17592186045487 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536006 1071 "![](images/b0/b0aa7d362596dcea976fead0467b9eb76c04b85c02c55dbd12e9a95652eda276.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y=\\sqrt{x}$ for $y\\in [0,1]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=3.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536006 1072 #uuid "fc2bc671-1b50-5524-a44c-d196ad9a937f" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536006 1074 "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=x & \\,⟹\\,x=y \\\\ y=\\sqrt{x} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f1/f15165acc18f4cf828671e3f9085274b26598a643be9eeb97be617272d7008d7.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x={y}^{2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={y}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/54/54820b3b386c9a435de04e0b3f07d4e2221a3e026ba5ba93ca25fa6640aa0ab1.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(3)-({y}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[3-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e1a6067500f81cabeac8dc43c4c585da608ea655667dc5e00f29a6fad0dda60.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(3)-(y)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[3-y]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({(3-{y}^{2})}^{2}-{(3-y)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{4}-7{y}^{2}+6y)\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{7{y}^{3}}{3}+3{y}^{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{{1}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{7\\cdot {1}^{3}}{3}+3\\cdot {1}^{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536007 1058 17592186536013 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536007 1062 #uuid "0727a7ae-2a4f-5a1a-b263-107fab74a519" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536007 1063 17592186253506 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536008 1058 17592186536032 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536008 1062 #uuid "09c7a56f-14c4-5bf0-b911-ecdd6881ed50" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536008 1063 17592186535978 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536009 1058 17592186536045 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536009 1062 #uuid "0c49baa4-622e-5e7f-9ddd-42763005e29c" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536009 1063 17592186535924 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536010 1062 #uuid "17e24c4d-fbe4-5267-b369-1dc13301b94c" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536010 1063 17592186373834 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536011 1058 17592186536036 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536011 1062 #uuid "1c1ad14e-74e6-51e6-a61f-0245fb4cfb09" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536011 1063 17592186308719 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536012 1062 #uuid "1ffd320b-d38b-57d0-b097-7c3039e0ec50" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536012 1063 17592186237874 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536013 1062 #uuid "20ef6a64-43e5-569e-9232-97b79c35df56" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536013 1063 17592186320880 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536014 1058 175921865360